what you touch stays reachable.

it is a find:

    f    by name
    s    by content
    m    by having been there

touching is the verbs that take a noun — open, edit, go there. see and
find touch nothing. the note is on the verb consuming its noun, not on
the noun changing.

    m          ranked, each address under its newest verb
    m n        the nth, address alone
    m kw...    the best-scoring match

history and memory

the log is history: what happened, in order. m is memory: what it is
worth now. one line per touch — when, verb, address — appended, never
rewritten. nothing derived, nothing updated in place.

`sh/c:/awk -v addr/` is where this breaks today. c rewrites the log so
an address appears once under its newest verb — a ranking decision taken
by the writer, and it spends the count to make it. after the rewrite,
thirty opens today and one open last month are the same single line, and
no reader tells them apart again. a writer that ranks throws away the
history it ranked from. c becomes an append and knows nothing else.

what it buys: the half-life is a number in one file, and changing it
re-scores everything already logged. get the split wrong and the same
change is a migration.

what a touch is worth

not per open. thirty opens today is one day's work, and a place touched
on ten days across a month is the habit — the burst must not bury it. so
credit is per address per day:

    score = sum over the days it was touched, 2^(-age in days / h)

h of seven: touched on one day only, it scores .91 the next day, .5
after a week, .05 after a month. five days spread over three weeks come
to about 2.2, so habit outranks burst by two to one, and neither count
nor recency is kept separately to say it.

forgetting is a floor on that score and not a rule of its own. below it
the address is not offered. `sh/w:/the log outlives/` stops curating —
no existence sweep, no newest-mention dedupe — and truncates the head,
which is invisible because what it cuts scores nothing.

the reader, whole:

    tac log | awk -v h=7 -v now=$now '
        { d = int((now - $1)/86400); k = $3; sub(/:[0-9]*$/,"",k)
          if (!seen[k,d]++) s[k] += 2^(-d/h)
          if (!v[k]) v[k] = $2 " " $3 }
        END { for (k in s) if (s[k] > .1) print s[k] "\t" v[k] }' |
        sort -rn | cut -f2

back is not memory

it is what blocks all of the above. bare m and m n are one list, so if
the order becomes score, m 1 stops being back — and back is exact or it
is nothing, never the highest-scoring other place.

back was never m's to hold. cur is per window and the log is one for
the machine, so m 1 already answers with another window's place. it
belongs beside cur: c keeps prev, per window, and back is c `{c -}. then
m drops the tmux id, the cur read, and m 0 — c bare is that — and is
read, rank, print. `sh/e:16`, `sh/o:33` and `sh/p:50` ask c instead.

only c writes prev. `fm/4-main/27-say.go` writes cur straight, on every
row, and must keep bypassing c or the log fills with browsing — so back
is the last address said out loud and not the last row passed over,
which is the one it is worth being.

that deletion is the point. the writer holds the fact and only the fact,
m holds what it is worth and only that.

